<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The agent's execution plane, outside the agent's process tree.

  Founder, 2026-09-13: "the agent shouldn't have raw, unrestricted bash spawned directly from its
  own process tree anyway. It should request execution from an isolated daemon." This is that
  daemon's launchd job.

  WHY IT IS SAFE TO LEAVE RUNNING, and why that is a design choice rather than an oversight:
  a port raised the cost to this machine's disk (measured 18.06 GiB, 5.6 GiB free) and the number
  the job service actually has is 60. So the daemon listens on a UNIX domain socket, is O(n) in the
  command, and costs nothing while idle: launchd starts it, and it blocks on accept().

  RunAtLoad brings it back after a reboot. KeepAlive restarts it if it dies, which is correct here
  and was correct to omit from ai.estate.idp: this process does NOT fork and exit; it serves, so
  a non-zero exit really is a death. Both were PROVEN 2026-09-20: kill -9 the daemon and launchd
  brought it back with a new pid inside 6s, socket and door working again.

  WHY ProgramArguments NAMES python3 AND NOT bin/exec-daemon. MEASURED 2026-09-20 on macOS 13.7.5:
  pointing launchd at the bash wrapper dies with exit 126
  `bash: .../bin/exec-daemon: Operation not permitted`. That is TCC refusing a launchd-spawned
  PROCESS EXECUTION of a file under ~/Documents -- it is not a property of this plist and not
  something a plist key can grant. The same launchd job running `python3 <daemon.py>` in the same
  directory works, because the exec target is /usr/local/bin/python3 and the protected path is
  only an argument. So the interpreter is named here and the daemon is named as its argument.

  This means the plist must be regenerated when the checkout moves: ${IDP} is substituted at
  install time by bin/idp-executor-agent. That is the price of naming an absolute path, and the
  alternative (a symlink the agent can retarget) trades a rebuild for a boundary hole.

  WHAT MAKES THE BOUNDARY REAL is ownership: the agent must not be able to edit this file or the
  daemon it names. It runs as the same uid today, so this is the half that can be built now, and
  `bin/idp-executor-status` reports which half is actually in place rather than leaving a
  half-boundary to read as a whole one. The other half is bin/idp-executor-install (a separate
  uid, a root LaunchDaemon, sudo, LAWS 54) -- NOT required for the daemon to run and serve.

  Install:    bin/idp-executor-agent
  Remove:     launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/ai.estate.executor
  Check:      bin/idp-executor-status
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>${HOME}</string>
	</dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>ai.estate.executor</string>
	<key>LowPriorityIO</key>
	<true/>
	<key>Nice</key>
	<integer>5</integer>
	<!-- /usr/local/bin/python3, NOT bin/exec-daemon. See the TCC note above: naming the bash
	     wrapper here is exit 126 on macOS 13. -->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>${PYTHON}</string>
		<string>${IDP}/platform/executor/daemon.py</string>
	</array>
	<key>WorkingDirectory</key>
	<string>${IDP}</string>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- This process serves rather than forks, so a death is a death. -->
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>10</integer>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>${HOME}/.estate/executor.out.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>${HOME}/.estate/executor.err.log</string>
	<!-- The execution plane never needs a shell of its own: it is handed a command and it bounds
	     that command. Launching it in a login shell would give it exactly the surface this
	     design exists to remove. -->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Interactive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
